Built-in spawn runtimes: `agy`, `claude`, `codex`, `gemini`, `muse`, `qwen`
(plus any runtime declared under `spawn.runtimes`). Gemini CLI 0.54.4 is
deprecated in favour of Antigravity, but both remain supported.

```bash
task-board spawn TASK-ID --role developer --background --agent agy --model gemini-3.6-flash-high --timeout 35m
```

`agy` takes no `--reasoning-effort`; effort is encoded in its eight scoped
model IDs. Minimum `agy` is 1.1.12; 1.1.14 is validated. Prompt delivery has
a 96 KiB argv budget; `SUCCESS`/`ERROR` JSON overrides process exit. Only the
top-level worker is tracked. See [README](README.md) for the full models,
flags, JSON, safety, configuration, and Gemini compatibility contract.
# Tracked Background Spawn

Canonical operator-facing contract for tracked background spawn runs. Covers `task-board spawn`, observation, control, fallback manual launch patterns, failure modes, and working habits.

**Primary interface:** `task-board spawn TASK-ID --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — wraps an explicit agent selection with automatic board integration.

**Context plane:** with `features.context_sharing` on, a spawn also resolves a
shared context. No flag means deterministic auto selection; repeated `--context`
names the primary and its supplementals in binding order; `--no-context`
suppresses the lookup and is mutually exclusive with `--context`;
`--register-context project|role|task` is the only publication opt-in, and
`project` requires the canonical `context-builder` role. Selection is gated on
what this machine can actually serve: a `ready` record whose provider handle is
not here — published by a colleague, or evicted by `materialization_idle_ttl` —
is dropped from auto selection with a `context_not_materialized` rejection and
the run starts cold, while an explicitly named `--context` is refused instead.
The registry record is never mutated by that verdict; the snapshot is intact on
the machine that holds it. The child receives
`TASK_BOARD_CONTEXT_ID` for its own writable fork and nothing else. A run that
holds a writable child executes inside a Session Manager session — a prompt-mode
launch would inherit nothing — and it needs no delivery goal to do so: the
context plane owns that run on its own, exactly as the goal plane owns a
`--goal-scope` run. Inspect the decision before launching with
`task-board q 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER)'`.
**Canonical scope:** README and SKILL.md should summarize spawn behavior, not redefine it. For selection semantics see [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](../.specs/spawn-model-selection.md); for durable runner/wait semantics see [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](../.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).

---

## Session Manager owner boundary

`task-board codex` and `task-board claude` are thin clients of the board-keyed
Task-board Session Manager. `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic and renders
the non-launching primary-session plan. The manager owns provider lifetime and
native reflection for both parent and spawned owners.

| Owner | Ledger slot | Provider session |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Primary wrapper | `parent/primary` | Manager-held Codex app-server/thread or Claude PTY |
| Tracked spawn | `run/RUN-*` | Distinct manager-held provider host for that run |

The run receives no manager endpoint, provider URL, thread/session identity, or
credential. Attach reconciles the exact v2 owner head before the assignment
turn. Provider parity covers requirement delivery, directives, recovery,
handoff evidence, acceptance, and audit; provider mechanics remain private to
the Codex and Claude host adapters.

---

## What Is Tracked Background Spawn

Tracked background spawn starts a real agent runtime without blocking the
parent/orchestrator shell. Ordinary prompt-mode runs use `claude -p`, `codex
exec`, or Qwen stream-JSON; goal-bound Codex/Claude owners execute inside a
manager-held session. Every run gets a durable `RUN-...` handle, persisted
state, replayable events, watcher output, a wait barrier, directives, logs, and
completion hooks.

The parent agent is the active Orchestrator role (`orchestrator` / legacy `coordinator`). It creates or selects board tasks, spawns child runs, watches their progress, sends directives when needed, and reads child outcome resources for routing. It should not turn into the worker or reviewer just because a task is small.

**Use cases:**
- Delegate implementation to a separate agent process with its own context window
- Run agents on different model families (Claude vs OpenAI) for diversity
- Scale beyond runtime-native helper slots while preserving board tracking
- Preserve nested spawn capability across parent/child agent processes

---

## Spawning via `task-board spawn` (Recommended)

The `spawn` command is the default way to launch tracked background runs. It handles assignment, prompt composition, persistence, logging, events, and completion hooks automatically.

**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex, Qwen, and Claude additionally require a model-compatible `--reasoning-effort`, each from its own effort vocabulary. The role drives agent identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults of its own. Role resolution precedes ceiling lookup and board access: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, the legacy `coordinator` spelling shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown names fail closed with typed errors.

**A project may waive `--model` for one role.** `spawn.ceilings.<provider>.roles.<role>.default_model` and `.default_reasoning_effort` are per-role selection defaults: they seed only the dimensions the caller omitted, an explicit flag always wins, and the seeded pair is resolved by exactly the same ceiling that bounds an explicit one. A ceiling is not a default — it bounds a selection the caller already made and cannot produce one — so configuring a bound never removes the flag. See [per-role model preferences](task-board-config.md#per-role-model-preferences) for the rules, the refusals, and the `spawn-policy-v2` rationale gate that a default does not waive.

Before spawning, resolve only the target policy:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
```

The projection returns the effective provider policy/schedule, target role
ceiling, target role selection default, selection mode, composition status, and
local queue capacity without auth/session/version-control data.
`resolved_role_default.spawns_without_manual_selection` reports whether that role
can be spawned for that provider without `--model`. Query `models(agent=<selected-agent>)` only
when the registered set is needed to resolve a model. Full `project_config()`
is the audit/debug surface.

## Provider System And UTC Schedule

`spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is the canonical provider policy. It is an
enforced tagged union: `exclusive` contains one provider, while `mixed`
contains at least two unique normalized providers. Explicit `--agent` wins over
affinity only inside that set. Otherwise exclusive pins its provider and mixed
keeps the current Codex/Claude runtime affinity only when the set allows it.
Out-of-set explicit providers or affinities are refused before task lookup,
assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch.

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "preferred_agentic_system": {
      "mixed": ["claude", "codex"]
    },
    "schedule": {
      "windows": [
        {
          "start_utc": "17:00",
          "end_utc": "03:00",
          "preferred_agentic_system": {
            "exclusive": "qwen"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

Schedule bounds are UTC `HH:MM`, start-inclusive/end-exclusive, and disjoint.
Later-to-earlier bounds cross midnight; equal or overlapping bounds are
invalid. An active window replaces the base provider system before provider
and registered-model validation. Outside every window, the base system applies;
if no base exists, ordinary runtime affinity remains unrestricted. The selected
provider's ceiling is resolved only after this step.

`project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is the currently effective
`{mode, allowed_providers, source, config_key}` projection.
`project_config().spawn.schedule` separately reports `timezone: "UTC"`, all
normalized windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system`.
Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains readable and projects to an effective
exclusive system, but startup diagnoses it with the canonical replacement.
The misspelling `preferred_agent_system` is not an alias.

## Project MCP Launch Composition

Tracked children inherit the project's enabled MCP servers through the versioned `agents-infra.child-launch-composition` contract. This capability is default-on:

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "launch_composition": {
      "enabled": true
    }
  }
}
```

Omitting either `launch_composition` or `enabled` means `true`; explicit `false` is the only opt-out. Local mode reads `spawn.launch_composition.enabled`. Remote mode reads `remote.spawn.launch_composition.enabled`, with the existing whole-policy root fallback only when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. A present remote policy with the field omitted defaults on instead of inheriting the root field. `project_config().spawn.launch_composition` reports the effective boolean, active dotted key, contract, schema version `1`, and `bare_with_evidence` degradation policy without invoking infra.

After agent selection and work-directory resolution, each ordinary prompt-mode
spawn invokes the non-launching child composition command **once**, for the
directory the child will actually run in:

```bash
agents-infra compose --agent qwen --project "$EXECUTION_ROOT" --schema-version 1 --json
```

`$EXECUTION_ROOT` is the Story worktree when the run has one, and the control
root otherwise. Composing the control root and then running the child elsewhere
would hand it MCP servers scoped to a directory it never opens, so the project
follows the execution root rather than the observation root. Task-board then
validates that the envelope names that same canonical project.

Task-board validates the contract identity, canonical project, selected provider, required metadata, and the complete provider allowlist before using any prefix. Codex accepts only `-c mcp_servers.<name>.(url|bearer_token_env_var|command|args)=...` pairs. Claude and Qwen accept only one `--mcp-config` JSON value rooted at `mcpServers`, with the contract's HTTP or stdio shapes. Any extra top-level flag, primary-session policy, unknown field, server mismatch, malformed JSON, or literal bearer value rejects the whole prefix; task-board never salvages individual arguments.

The accepted prefix is prepended at the provider's top-level boundary for
ordinary prompt-mode launches. A goal-bound Codex/Claude owner instead requires
`agents-infra compose --mode primary-session`; task-board validates and
persists that exact managed-host plan for the Session Manager. Qwen does not
support goal-bound owners. Restart, reroute, and autonomous recovery successors
retain the plan and rebind only the new `RUN-*` owner context.

| Condition | Child launch | Sanitized status |
|---|---|---|
| Explicit opt-out | Bare | `disabled` |
| Valid prefix with MCP servers | Composed | `applied` |
| Valid empty MCP set | Bare-equivalent | `empty` |
| `agents-infra` missing | Bare | `degraded_tool_missing` |
| Old/pre-contract command | Bare | `degraded_contract_unavailable` |
| Unsupported contract/schema | Bare | `degraded_unsupported_schema` |
| Recognized composition failure | Bare | `degraded_compose_failed` |
| Malformed, mismatched, or disallowed payload | Bare | `degraded_invalid_payload` |

Degradation is fail-open only for this optional capability: it never weakens or rewrites board-owned safety flags. `spawn --json` returns the safe summary under `launchComposition`; task notes and durable run state/events carry the same snake-case summary. The private manifest alone stores the validated MCP argv snapshot needed by the detached runner. Public evidence never contains argv payloads, URLs, commands, command arguments, headers, external stderr, or environment values.

`bearer_token_env_var` is a name reference, not a value lookup. Task-board and agents-infra do not resolve it during composition. The child inherits unrelated caller environment variables through the existing provider filters, while evidence retains only referenced variable names. Do not put token values in task prompts, notes, resources, manifests, events, logs, or command diagnostics.

## Story Worktree Isolation

A repository-mutating producer spawn does not run in the shared checkout. It
runs in a Git worktree scoped to its containing Story, so concurrent producers
in independent Stories stop clobbering one working tree and each Story's commits
stay clean and buildable on their own.

| Concern | Value |
|---|---|
| Isolation unit | One managed worktree and branch per Story |
| Branch | `task-board/story/<STORY-ID>`, forked from trunk, short-lived |
| Path | `<control-root>/.temp/<STORY-ID>/worktree` |
| Delivery model | Trunk-based; the complete review-accepted Story lands directly on trunk |
| Parallelism | Independent Stories run concurrently; one exclusive lease serializes writers inside a Story |
| Ownership | task-board owns Git state; provider-native worktree flags are never used |

Delivery is trunk-based development with short-lived Story branches and no
partial landing: an internal Task checkpoint never reaches trunk on its own, and
there is no feature flag that turns isolation off. How a Story lands as commits
is the commit-owning mover's own call — see
[statuses](statuses.md#commit-confirmation-for-accepted-work).
Configure trunk with `spawn.worktree_isolation.integration_base_branch`, or
omit it for remote-neutral discovery: the one branch a remote HEAD and
matching tracking config jointly and uniquely name — discovery reads every
configured remote and never assumes one is called `origin` — or, only when
the repository has no remote at all, the control root's current non-detached
branch. Before the selected branch is trusted for provisioning, checkpoint, or
integration, its configured upstream (when it has one) is fetched and compared
against the local tip by exact OID: an ahead or diverged local tip, or an
unreadable/ambiguous authority, refuses rather than proceeding from unproven
state. A branch with positively no upstream configured uses its own local OID
directly, with no fetch. See the
[canonical config reference](task-board-config.md#story-worktree-isolation)
for the full discovery order and refusal table.

### Control root versus execution root

The old single work directory is split, and the split is what keeps a wave
observable from one place:

- **Control root** — the project root. Owns the authoritative board, roles,
  prompts, logwork, `.temp/spawn-runs`, directives, scheduler locks, the
  workspace registry, and `spawn.max_parallel`. Every observation command reads
  here.
- **Execution root** — `<control-root>/.temp/<STORY-ID>/worktree`. The child's
  working directory and the project passed to agents-infra composition, which
  runs once, for the execution root.

The run manifest keeps `work_dir` as a backward-compatible control-root alias
and adds explicit `control_root` and `execution_root`. RUN state and events
carry the Story id and a **sanitized** workspace identity — the
control-root-relative path plus opaque workspace/repository ids. The absolute
execution path exists only in the private manifest under the gitignored
`.temp/spawn-runs/` tree.

Children are pointed at the authoritative board explicitly, with an absolute
`TASK_BOARD_DIR`. A linked worktree contains its own `.task-board` checkout
copy; that copy is a checkout artifact, not the board. Do not run `task-board`
against it, and do not override `TASK_BOARD_DIR`.

Restart, reroute, and recovery successors inherit the workspace rather than
provisioning a second one. For a workspace-bound run the control root and the
execution root are one pair: a reroute `--workdir` override is **rejected**
rather than applied, because the registry record and the Story lease live under
the control root while the child writes in the execution root, and moving one
without the other would let two runs each believe they hold the lease for the
same worktree. Respawn the Story instead. A run with no Story workspace still
accepts the override.

### Which runs get a workspace

| Run | Workspace |
|---|---|
| `orchestrator` / `coordinator` | No — stays in the control root |
| `task_class=metadata` | No — control-plane only, must not edit repository files |
| `code`, `docs`, `research` producers | Yes |
| Reviewer of such a leaf | Yes — the same workspace, holding the same lease so the candidate cannot drift during review |
| Element with no containing Story | No — there is no Story-scoped workspace to provision |

The first three rows are decided facts about what the run *is*, so they produce
no workspace and the spawn proceeds in the control root. A repository question is
not one of them. When a run in the "Yes" rows has no repository to isolate in,
the spawn **refuses** rather than quietly falling back to the shared checkout:

| Control root | Result |
|---|---|
| Not a Git working tree | `worktree_not_a_git_repository`, before assignment, prompt, RUN, or child |
| Git state unreadable (git missing, probe failed) | `worktree_git_command_failed` — unknown, not absent |

Both refusals are evaluated after the §9.1 local-board capability precondition,
so a resolved remote board still reports `worktree_isolation_requires_local_board`
first and an operator is never sent to fix a repository that was never the
blocker.

### Scheduling

A run waiting for a Story lease stays `queued` and **consumes no
`spawn.max_parallel` slot**, and it does not block independent Stories queued
behind it. Admission takes Story eligibility first and a global slot second, and
releases both on every terminal path.

### Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint

A producer that hands off inside a managed workspace publishes a **Change
Request**: a revisioned record attached to the existing Task/Bug/Story, not a new
board element. It carries the branch, the base and candidate tree OIDs, a binary
patch resource with its SHA-256, the changed-path list, and an explicit
`repository_delta` of `present` or `empty`.

The candidate is snapshotted through a temporary alternate Git index, so tracked
changes, deletions, and untracked non-ignored files are all captured **without
touching the managed worktree's own index**. Every `.task-board/**` entry is then
restored from the base tree, which is why a `changed_paths` list can never
contain a board path — the authoritative board is the control root, and a
worktree's `.task-board` copy is a checkout artifact. Paths dropped by that rule
are recorded as `dropped_board_paths` and shown by `worktree status` and in the
reviewer's prompt. A non-empty list is a diagnostic, usually a `git add -A` in
the child, not a failure.

Construction runs for **every** producer completion in a managed workspace,
whether or not the run carries a delivery goal. Managed workspace activation
turns on role, task class, containing Story and local board alone, so an ordinary
`task-board spawn TASK-... --role developer --background` gets an isolated
worktree — and the half of the feature that makes that worktree reviewable
activates on exactly the same conditions.

It runs **after** the spawn completion artifact guard, and on the same two facts:
the board reached the run's end status, and the run attached a new or updated
task-scoped outcome artifact. A run that attached nothing is reverted to its
producer status by that guard and leaves no Change Request at all. A read that
FAILS is reported as a failure and never as "did not hand off".

A workspace-claiming producer that publishes nothing says why, on the run's event
log and in a board note: `handoff_unsatisfied`, with the detail — the board is
not at the end status, or no task-scoped outcome artifact was attached.

The other two reasons are recorded nowhere, on one rule: a reason that is true of
every run of its class says nothing about the run in front of you, and repeating
it on every completion buries the case the reporting exists for. An un-isolated
run skips with `no_managed_workspace`, which is true of every un-isolated run. A
reviewer-archetype run skips with `not_a_producer`, which is true of every
reviewer run — a reviewer judges a candidate rather than publishing one, so
noting it would append a line describing correct behaviour to the element's
progress on every single review cycle.

`repository_delta=empty` means the producer changed no repository file. It is an
attribute, never a state and never terminal: the Change Request is `ready` like
any other, a reviewer **is** spawned, and the injected prompt says in plain terms
that nothing changed and lists the outcome resources that were produced. Deciding
whether that was correct is a judgement about intent versus delivery, which is
what the reviewer archetype is for.

**The kind is derived from the board, never declared.** A Change Request is
`task_delta` or `story_final`, and the second is what `worktree integrate`
accepts as the Story's integration unit. Publication asks the board whether
completing this leaf would also close its Story — the same question
`worktree checkpoint` asks to REFUSE that leaf, through the same call — so the
leaf one command declines is exactly the leaf the other treats as the Story's
unit. Every indeterminate answer refuses the publication: no board access, an
unreadable board, or an element the board does not contain means the question was
never answered, and answering `task_delta` on a failed read would leave the Story
permanently unintegrable with nothing recording why.

**The base follows the kind.** A `task_delta` is measured from the Story branch
tip, where earlier leaves already checkpointed. A `story_final` is measured from
the workspace record's **current base OID**, initially the exact selected base
OID produced by upstream freshness resolution and later advanced only by a
successful final-leaf refresh. It is never recomputed through the local trunk
ref: that ref may remain behind the upstream that provisioning fetched, which
would expand the patch to already-merged predecessor work. The candidate tree
is a full snapshot, so the recorded base is also the stable input to later
re-parenting when trunk moves again.

**A Story has at most one unaccepted producer candidate.** The worktree is
Story-scoped and the snapshotter has no durable per-task path ownership model.
Before a producer starts, spawn therefore scans the Story's Change Requests and
refuses `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` when another element owns a
`draft`, `ready`, `reviewing`, `changes_requested`, `stale`, or `conflicted`
revision. The refusal names the blocking task and is checked both before
provisioning and after lease admission to close the concurrent-spawn race.
Reviewers bypass the producer gate, and the same element may start rework for
its own revision.

**The producer runs the landing suite and records tree-bound evidence.** When
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` is configured, publication runs it
in the Story worktree and records `validation` keyed to the published candidate
tree. The suite runs against the WORKTREE while the evidence claims a TREE, so
the candidate is recomputed afterwards and compared; a suite that moved the
worktree — a build that drops artifacts, a codegen step, a formatter — yields
**no** evidence rather than evidence keyed to a tree it never saw. A failing
suite is fail-sticky at its first non-zero command and cannot publish a `ready`
candidate: the immutable revision is immediately `changes_requested`, its
bounded transcript is attached as a task-scoped system resource, and validation
records the one-based failed command index/total plus the real exit status. The
producer run follows the existing non-success/recovery-successor path with a
retry step naming that resource. A command that could not run at all is never
recorded as a pass. Passing and explicitly empty configured suites retain the
ready/success path. See
[how the evidence binds](#validation-binds-to-the-tree-that-lands).

Before the **final unresolved leaf's** producer starts, task-board refreshes the
clean Story checkpoint branch onto current trunk, so that producer works and
validates against the tree that will actually land. The refresh consumes the
exact authoritative OID returned by the immediately preceding provisioning
fetch; it never resolves the workspace record's durable local base ref, which
may legitimately remain behind that fetched upstream. It refreshes nothing else: a
non-final leaf's candidate is a delta on the branch tip, and moving the fork point
under it would rewrite the base of work already reviewed. The refresh never fails
a spawn and never forces anything. A conflict aborts and leaves the branch exactly
as it was, routing to same-branch rework; a workspace holding uncommitted work is
skipped entirely, because a rebase under uncommitted work is the operation that
destroys work existing nowhere else. Both outcomes are reported on the element and
leave the recorded base untouched; only a successful refresh records the new
reviewed trunk OID.

Reviewer spawn recomputes the candidate first. A worktree that moved marks the
revision `stale` and refuses the spawn: task-board never reviews a moving
candidate.

The reviewer records exactly one verdict. Acceptance is a named mutation:

```bash
task-board m 'accept_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>, evidence=<ELEMENT-ID>_review-verdict.md)'
```

Only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may run it. `accept_cr`
resolves the caller's tracked run, loads its manifest, and requires a
reviewer-archetype run bound to this element and this revision; an untracked
caller, a producer run, a reviewer bound to another element or another revision,
and a manifest that could not be read are all refused with
`change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`.

The `evidence` must also be **this run's own**: a task-scoped outcome resource
that did not exist when the reviewer launched, or whose content changed since.
The producer's `_results.md` sits on the element before the reviewer is spawned,
and naming it would record "reviewer run X accepted revision N with evidence E"
without a reviewer-authored judgement. The live merged task/reviewer checklist
must also be complete. Identity, revision, and fresh-evidence checks retain
precedence; checklist refusal then occurs before any CR, status, or activity
write. Unchanged,
unproven (a legacy manifest with names but no digests), and unreadable evidence
are all refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`.

Acceptance is an attestation, and
`worktree checkpoint` treats the accepted record as its sole authorization to
close the leaf with `commit_ack=scope_committed` — a self-minted acceptance would
put a commit acknowledgement behind a review that never happened.

It sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, and parks the
element at `to-review` — the **accepted handoff**, not `done`. For a
`story_final` revision it also records the **reviewed trunk OID**, which is the
record's own base: that is the trunk the reviewer's diff was taken against, and
integration reads it twice — the control root's `HEAD` must equal it, and the
paths trunk advanced since it decide whether the acceptance survives. The value
is **checked before it is recorded**: the base must be contained in trunk's
history, and a base that is not, or a repository that could not answer, refuses
the acceptance rather than writing an unverified claim into a field two gates
trust. That containment check uses the exact authority OID returned by the fresh
trunk resolution; it never re-reads a durable local branch ref that may
legitimately remain behind its fetched upstream. The CR acceptance runs inside
the same board/activity transaction callback as the `to-review` transition, so
a CR refusal rolls task and ancestor statuses back and publishes no activity.
A `task_delta` records no reviewed trunk OID at all — its base is a Story
branch tip, which is not a trunk commit and never becomes one. Accepted work never
stays in `reviewing`: a reviewer run that ends at bare `reviewing` with no
accepted Change Request is still cancelled and respawned, and the accepted branch
is derived from the record rather than from the status. `accept_cr` takes no
acknowledgement argument. `changes_requested` routes to `to-dev` or `analysis`
and the next producer publishes revision N+1 on the same branch and worktree;
prior revisions stay readable as immutable evidence. Stop-The-Line is unchanged.

A reviewer that is cancelled and respawned keeps its Change Request binding. The
successor inherits the revision, the CR id and the diff digest along with the
Story workspace and the prompt, because the prompt it reuses tells it to run
`accept_cr` on that revision — a successor handed no revision is the exact shape
`accept_cr` refuses, so the recovery ladder would run to its attempt limit and
park. A successor **rerouted to a different archetype** drops the binding
instead, and says so in its queue summary: a producer publishes revision N+1 by
reading the store, and it was never handed a candidate to accept.

The Orchestrator then closes a **non-final** leaf:

```bash
task-board worktree checkpoint <ELEMENT-ID>
```

That commits the accepted candidate tree as one internal commit on the Story
branch under a compare-and-swap on the branch tip, records it on the workspace,
and moves the leaf to `done`. Nothing reaches trunk: internal checkpoints only
delimit later Task diffs and make work recoverable, and the final Story squash
discards them. A `repository_delta=empty` revision creates no commit — its tree
would equal its parent's — and the leaf still reaches `done` by the same command.
A replayed checkpoint creates no second commit. A drifted worktree, a branch that
moved, and an unaccepted revision each refuse with a typed error and leave the
board and the branch exactly as they were.

The internal commit is always `git commit-tree -S`, because `commit-tree` does
not honor `commit.gpgsign`. Git resolves the repository's configured
author/committer identity and signing key. A signer failure occurs before the
branch CAS and before the Change Request or board transition; there is no
unsigned fallback.

**A checkpoint never closes the Story.** If the leaf is the Story's last open
child, moving it to `done` would promote the Story in the same mutation, and the
blanket `commit_ack=scope_committed` would then satisfy the version-control gate
for a Story whose branch has provably never reached trunk. That is refused with
`change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint`, naming
`task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>` — the step that lands the branch
before making the transition. The rule is about scope, not acknowledgement: it
applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it runs
before the drift check, the commit, the record transition and the status change,
so the accepted revision survives for the integration path to use. A board that
cannot be read, or an element the board does not contain, refuses for the same
reason — the checkpoint's scope is then unknown rather than clear.

**Checkpoint the leaves of a Story in the order their candidates were published.**
A checkpoint asserts that the branch tip still equals the revision's recorded
base. If leaf B's producer completes before leaf A is checkpointed, both
revisions carry the same base; checkpointing A moves the tip, and B then refuses
permanently with `change_request_checkpoint_conflict`. The refusal is correct and
leaves the branch recoverable, but the recovery is **republish revision N+1**,
not retry — base refresh is not part of this contract.

Change Request persistence is **local-board only**. `accept_cr` and Change
Request construction both refuse a resolved remote board with
`worktree_isolation_requires_local_board` before any record is written, any diff
resource is uploaded, or any element field is mutated. There is no remote Change
Request path and none is emulated.

### Landing a Story: `worktree integrate`

The Orchestrator closes a Story with:

```bash
task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID> --cr <ELEMENT-ID> --revision <N> \
  --commit-time <RFC3339>
```

It runs in the **control root** — the repository's main working tree, on the
configured trunk branch. No other checkout is ever advanced, and a control root
whose `HEAD` is an *ancestor* of trunk on a side branch refuses
`integration_checkout_not_trunk` rather than being fast-forwarded silently.

The command produces at most two commits: one squash commit carrying the
accepted candidate tree, and a second **board-only** commit titled
`Record <STORY-ID> board state`, staged by an explicit pathspec of exactly the
manifest paths and never by `git add -A`. Both carry the same resolved commit
timestamp on the author and the committer date; the commit-time contract itself
is owned by [statuses.md](statuses.md) and is not restated here. A Story whose
accepted candidate has no repository delta produces **no** Story commit and still
produces the board-only one — its board record is the Story's entire work
product.

All managed commit objects are explicitly signed with `-S`: the temporary
commit that materializes a revalidation tree, the final Story squash, and the
board-only commit. Checkpoint commits use the same rule. Identity/key selection
remains Git repository policy; task-board neither creates a signing key nor
substitutes an agent identity. A Story signer failure precedes transaction open
and ref movement. A board-only signer failure leaves HEAD and the durable phase
unchanged; already-frozen manifest/staging work remains retryable, and the next
run creates exactly one signed board commit. `version_control.confirm` governs
the separate `commit_ack` status gate and cannot disable signing.

#### What the board-state commit covers, and what it leaves alone

A Story branch **structurally cannot carry board bytes**. Candidate construction
restores every `.task-board/**` entry from the base tree, so a `changed_paths`
list can never contain a board path, and a board path appearing there anyway is
a loud failure (`board_code_overlap`) rather than a handled case. The board
delta therefore has to land through a separate commit, in the control root,
against the authoritative board.

That second commit stages an explicit pathspec bounded by
`board_paths(<STORY-ID>)` — the outer bound; the committed set is narrowed
further, as [the no-widen claim](#how-board-paths-are-admitted) states.
`board_paths(<STORY-ID>)` is exactly two classes:

- **Lane class** — the Story's own board directory subtree in its entirety
  (the Story and every descendant Task/Bug), plus `.task-board/.resources/<ID>`
  and the exact `.task-board/.activity/<ID>/events.ndjson` stream for every
  element in that subtree, including retained deleted descendants.
- **Shared class** — the ancestor aggregation files on this Story's chain: each
  ancestor's `progress.md` and exact activity stream. In the current layout
  that is the Epic's `progress.md` plus `.activity/<EPIC-ID>/events.ndjson`.

Everything else under `.task-board/` is outside the pathspec and stays exactly
as it was — ordinary uncommitted working-tree state that integration neither
examines, stages, nor commits. Concretely that is `.task-board/.goals/**`, every
other Story's subtree and their resource directories, and any other Epic. This
is deliberate rather than an oversight: those paths belong to other lanes, and a
Story's integration has no basis for deciding what state they should be in.
Checkpoint/integration status transitions append activity transactionally;
their runtime-minted event bytes are admitted only at those precomputed exact
member/ancestor paths. A foreign activity stream remains outside the pathspec
even when it is valid, dirty, and journaled.

A completed local `set_parent` into this Story adds one deliberately narrower
class: the exact README.md/progress.md tombstones under the moved element's old
directory. The filesystem move transaction supplies candidate hints only.
`worktree integrate` independently re-resolves the live destination from the
authoritative store, enumerates the old element payloads from Git HEAD, matches
their pre-move blob digests, and requires the old paths to be absent and
unowned. It never admits the source Story subtree, a caller-supplied path, or a
path merely because `.element-move-journal.json` names it. A crash is recovered
by re-running the same `set_parent` or `worktree integrate`; both operations are
forward-only and idempotent.

A pre-journal legacy move has no record to use even as a hint. Only the
destination Story's integration may recover it; `dropped_board_paths` from a
different Story is diagnostic and grants no cross-lane authority. Destination
integration requires the exact duplicate-in-HEAD shape: one same-named Task/Bug
source directory directly beneath a live Story, absent and unowned in the
authoritative control root, with exactly README.md and progress.md and a README
byte-identical to the store-rendered destination. Partial, ambiguous,
mismatched, or unreadable evidence refuses before the transaction opens. The
operator repairs the authoritative board if needed, targets the destination
Story, and reruns the same `worktree integrate` command; a hand-edited
transaction, manifest, or path list supplies no authority.

#### Validation binds to the tree that lands

**The landing gate is configured, and it defaults to refusing.** The gate reads
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`: the ordered command list that
must pass on the tree that lands. An **absent** suite refuses
`validation_not_configured` before any ref movement; a project that genuinely
has nothing to run states that as an explicitly present, empty list
(`{"commands": []}`), which is a recorded decision rather than an absent one.
The [canonical config reference](task-board-config.md#the-landing-gate-suite)
carries the full three-valued contract and the refused in-between shapes.

Evidence is keyed to a **tree OID**, not to a Change Request. That is the whole
point of the binding: re-parenting an accepted Change Request onto an advanced
trunk changes the candidate tree, which necessarily breaks the binding and
forces revalidation against the exact commit integration will land. A reviewer's
acceptance survives the re-parent; their validation evidence does not.

The producer records that evidence at Change Request build, and integration is
the second reader rather than the first. A Change Request that carries **no**
usable evidence — an unconfigured suite at publication time, a re-parented tree,
or a suite whose worktree moved under it — does not silently satisfy the gate
and does not silently fail it: integration runs the configured suite itself,
against the exact tree it is about to land, inside the integration lock.

**Three non-passing outcomes, kept apart on purpose.** Collapsing them would
send an operator to the wrong repair, and two of the three are not the
producer's fault at all:

| Outcome | Meaning | Is it a rework signal? |
|---|---|---|
| `validation_not_configured` | no suite is configured; nothing ran | **No** — configure the suite |
| `revalidation_failed` | the suite ran on the landing tree and failed | **Yes** — the only one that is |
| `revalidation_unavailable` | the suite could not be executed at all | **No** — not a pass either; integration stays blocked and retryable |

Two further codes are `unavailable`-class rather than failures:
`revalidation_materialization_failed`, when the merged tree could not be
materialized as the commit integration would land, and
`revalidation_stale_ref`, when a `refs/task-board/revalidate/<STORY-ID>` left by
a crashed attempt is present — that ref is never silently adopted and no suite
is run over it. Every one of these leaves trunk unmoved, board status unchanged
and acceptance intact. Repeated revalidation of one accepted revision is capped;
past the cap the revision is demoted to `stale`, which *is* a rework signal.

#### When trunk advanced under an accepted Change Request

Acceptance is invalidated by **changed paths, not by trunk's OID**. A Story that
was reviewed against a trunk that has since moved is not automatically stale;
whether the acceptance survives depends on what moved.

| Trunk since the reviewed OID | Result |
|---|---|
| Unchanged | Nothing to do; the accepted tree is the landing tree |
| Advanced, **no** path intersection with `changed_paths` | Three-way merge of the candidate onto trunk; on success the revision is **re-parented** — same revision, still `accepted`, `reparent_count` incremented — and revalidation is then forced because the candidate tree changed |
| Advanced, **intersecting** a path this Change Request also changes | `integration_base_moved`; the revision is demoted to `stale` — nobody has reviewed the combination |
| Advanced path-disjoint, but the three-way merge **conflicts** | `stale`, same route: rework and republish |
| Merged tree diffs against trunk to a **different path set** than the reviewer read | `stale`; the merge would have landed something outside the reviewed surface |

The path-intersection test is a fast reject, never a proof of mergeability —
renames, mode changes and directory/file transitions can conflict without a
name-level intersection — which is why the three-way merge runs anyway and its
resulting shape is compared against the paths the reviewer actually read.

A `stale` revision is ordinary rework: the producer republishes revision N+1 on
the same branch and worktree, and a new reviewer accepts it. Nothing about a
stale acceptance moves trunk, board status, or the recorded workspace.

Two adjacent refusals belong to the same family. `integration_checkout_busy`
reports a merge, rebase or cherry-pick in progress in the control root, and
`integration_blocked` reports a checkout integration may not act on at all: trunk
checked out elsewhere, a non-empty index, or a dirty path that overlaps the
Story's landing paths. Overlapping local edits are refused rather than
stashed, committed, or overwritten.

#### How board paths are admitted

**Admission is decided by the Orchestrator, from content it re-derives itself.**
A board path may be committed only when the bytes in the control root equal what
the authoritative store renders for the owning element — content equality
against the store's own rendering, computed by the integrating process under its
own lock — and only when nothing under the Story's board paths moved inside the
integration window that the transaction cannot account for, which is enforced as
a compare-and-swap over that window. A path whose bytes the store does not
reproduce, or whose presence contradicts ownership, refuses
`board_path_content_unrecognized`; drift inside the window refuses
`board_delta_moved`.

**Nothing a child authors decides any of this.** Local boards keep a board write
journal at `.task-board/.board-write-ledger.json` so a process killed mid-write
can be resolved by content afterwards. It is **non-authorizing**: no admission
decision and no membership decision reads it, and deleting every record in it
changes no outcome. There is likewise **no lane attestation and no per-lane
board-write authority** — no record by which a lane claims the right to a board
byte, and none is emulated. Three revisions of the design tried to make such a
record unforgeable and none could, because every input lives inside the trust
domain of the process it would attest.

**What the no-widen claim is, stated narrowly.** The committed board path set is
contained in `board_paths(<STORY-ID>)` intersected with the transaction's
`precondition_delta`, the transition element's own escalation projection, and
the corresponding exact activity-stream projection for those status writes.
Content is whatever the authoritative store renders at freeze or recovery time.
The command does **not** claim to reproduce the byte state at the instant of the
transition, and **it does not claim to identify which lane wrote a board byte**.
It claims exactly two things: every byte committed is a byte the store
reproduces, and nothing under the Story's board paths changed inside the
integration window that the transaction cannot account for.

If a lane path drifts after the manifest is frozen, integration refuses
`board_delta_moved` naming the `integrated_board_uncommitted` condition: the
Story commit is already on trunk and its board delta is not. The transaction
stays at `board_state_pending`, so the Story is not integrated and its workspace
is not reaped. Resolve the drift and re-run `worktree integrate`; it resumes the
recorded transaction rather than starting a second one. Note that no command
prints the string `integrated_board_uncommitted` — the surface that reports the
transaction and its phase is `task-board worktree transaction show <STORY-ID>`.

**Crash recovery is re-running the same command.** One durable transaction
record per Story carries the phase; re-running resumes it rather than starting a
second integration. A rollback is offered in exactly one phase — `prepared` with
trunk unmoved — because that is the only phase with anything to roll back to;
every later phase resumes forward and none undoes a landed commit.

**A fresh accepted final revision opens a new generation, not a second live
transaction.** The active generation is supersedable only at `cleanup_pending`,
or at `discharged` after the discharge postcondition is recomputed. Integration
reads and verifies the newly requested accepted CR binding before choosing this
path, and independently proves the old generation from Git plus its durable
manifest; neither caller claims, Story status, nor a hand-written phase grants
rotation. Prepared, in-flight, transition-refused, integration-abandoned,
unreadable, and unproved records remain active and refuse.

Before generation N+1 becomes active, generation N is preserved create-only at
`.temp/integration-txn/archive/<STORY-ID>/generation-N.json`. Activation then
atomically replaces the active pointer. A crash between those operations leaves
the archived generation and the same old active record, so the ordinary retry
revalidates both and completes activation. Concurrent attempts serialize on the
repository integration lock and converge on the same generation. A command that
names the active CR revision remains an idempotent resume and never rotates it.

**What re-validates the transition binding, and what only looks like it does.**
`transition_element_id` is immutable transaction identity and is re-checked at
every recovery. The check that decides it is an identity comparison against the
transaction's `cr_id`: the Change Request store keys revisions by element id, so
resolving the record *through* `transition_element_id` and then comparing the two
is a tautology that cannot fail, and a binding rewritten to a same-Story sibling
that owns its own accepted `story_final` Change Request passes every other gate.
Pinning the resolved record to the `cr_id` captured at open is what refuses it,
with `txn_transition_binding_invalid`. Recovery never falls back to a Story-wide
projection on a bad binding.

### The transition lease, and what it does not do

While an integration sits at `board_transition_pending` or `transition_refused`,
a board mutation whose own write set intersects that transaction's lane paths
refuses `element_under_integration` unless the caller passes the matching
`txn_id=` argument. A mutation disjoint from those paths still succeeds — a
lease that blocks unrelated work is an outage, not a gate.

**The lease authorizes nothing, and no gate depends on it.** Its token is
caller-supplied, exactly like `commit_ack`, and this design does not claim it is
unforgeable. The gate is the recovery classifier, which consults no
caller-supplied input at all; the lease is availability. The lease never expires
on a timer, because a TTL reintroduces the window it closes — so a crashed
integration holds its lane paths until it resumes or an operator disposes of it.
`worktree transaction show <STORY-ID>` is the surface that reports it;
`worktree status` describes the Story *workspace* — its record, presence,
branch, Change Requests, dropped board paths and Story lease — and carries no
integration-transaction field.

**On a remote board the predicate is constantly false and the check is a
no-op.** There are no integration transactions to hold a lease, so nothing is
refused and nothing is enforced. That is stated here rather than left implied,
because an unstated no-op on one surface is a silent bypass. The managed
workspace path is local-board only in any case: `worktree integrate` refuses a
remote board with `worktree_isolation_requires_local_board` before the
integration lock and before the transaction opens, and the recorded rationale is
data locality — no single process holds both operands of the admission
comparison — not authority.

### What `pre_digest` and `post_digest` compare, and what they deliberately ignore

The recovery classifier decides whether sub-step 6c ran by rendering every
planned lane path from the authoritative store and asking which of the two
recorded states it is in. That comparison is over the rendering **with the
`## Last Update` stamp normalized away**, not over exact bytes, and the reason is
load-bearing rather than cosmetic.

`renderProgressContent` stamps `## Last Update` from the wall clock. Under an
exact-bytes comparison, only a writer that pinned that clock to this
transaction's `resolved_commit_time` could ever render `post_digest` — which
makes the digest a **writer-identity** test, and writer identity is exactly the
model the design withdrew. Two required behaviours are unreachable under it:

- an identical *foreign* transition renders the same content and must be
  **adopted**, because the transaction's postcondition is that the board holds
  this state and it does;
- an operator repair through the ordinary mutation surface must be able to reach
  the planned post-state, or `resolve --resolution repaired` can never succeed
  and the only exit from `transition_refused` is `incomplete-integration`.

Normalization is performed by the store's own progress parser and writer, so an
unknown or hand-added section survives untouched and still separates `ALIEN`
from `POST`. Content that is not a progress file is unaffected: its Last Update
never changed, so nothing is rewritten and the digest is the exact one.

Everything else keeps **exact bytes**, because every other comparison is between
two views of the same file rather than between a plan and a later rendering:
board-path admission, the phase A/B window CAS, the phase C staged-blob check,
and the "the blob in `HEAD` equals the store's current rendering" half of the
discharge postcondition.

### Disposing of a refused or abandoned integration

```bash
task-board worktree transaction show      <STORY-ID>
task-board worktree transaction resolve   <STORY-ID> --refusal <digest> \
  --resolution repaired|incomplete-integration --reason TEXT
task-board worktree transaction discharge <STORY-ID> --refusal <digest> --reason TEXT
```

These are the **only** exits from `transition_refused` and
`integration_abandoned`, and they are forward-only: none of them undoes a landed
commit or rewrites trunk. `integrate`, `worktree repair`, `worktree gc` and
cleanup all re-report a recorded refusal verbatim and change nothing.

**Read both phases against what is already on trunk.** Both are reached from
`board_transition_pending`, which is *after* the Story's squash commit landed.
So for a Story in either phase the repository work is on trunk and stays there;
what is unfinished is the board state. `transition_refused` means an automatic
path hit a refusal it will not guess past and stopped. `integration_abandoned`
means an operator has declared that this integration will not complete —
a decision about the transaction, never a retraction of the commit.

| Command | Precondition | Typed refusals |
|---|---|---|
| `transaction show` | none; prints nothing when no transaction is recorded | — |
| `transaction resolve` | phase is `transition_refused` | `txn_not_refused`, `txn_refusal_digest_mismatch`, `txn_repair_incomplete`, `transaction_disposition_not_operator` |
| `transaction discharge` | phase is `integration_abandoned` | `txn_not_abandoned`, `txn_refusal_digest_mismatch`, `txn_discharge_postcondition_unmet`, `transaction_disposition_not_operator` |

All three are local-board only, like every other managed-workspace surface.
`transaction_disposition_not_operator` refuses a disposition attempted from
inside a spawned run — a guardrail against an accidental child disposal, not a
boundary (see below).

A recorded refusal carries the **evidence it was decided from** — the code, the
offending path, the digest that was observed and the value that was expected —
and not just a sentence. That is what makes `show` actionable: an operator
deciding between `repaired` and `incomplete-integration` needs to see which path
disagreed and by how much, and a refusal reduced to prose would send them to
re-derive it. For a plan entry refused as `txn_plan_degenerate` the observed and
expected digests are the *same* value, and that identity is the finding: an entry
whose pre-state and post-state are equal classifies as both `PRE` and `POST`, so
recovery could resume it as "already applied" when the mutation never ran.

**Neither `--refusal` nor the absent-`TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` check authorizes
anything.** Both are caller-supplied guardrails, exactly like `commit_ack` and
the lease token, and neither is implemented or documented as unforgeable.
`--refusal` proves the operator is disposing of the refusal that is on disk right
now rather than one they read before it changed; the environment check guards
against a spawned child disposing by accident.

The boundary that matters is that **two of the three outcomes cannot be obtained
by assertion at all**:

- `--resolution repaired` re-runs the recovery classifier under the board
  mutation lock and decides on that, never on the operator's claim. Anything
  short of the planned post-state on every lane path is `txn_repair_incomplete`,
  with the phase unmoved, the original refusal still authoritative and the lease
  still held. A hand-written phase field buys nothing either, because the next
  phase re-classifies on entry.
- `discharge` recomputes the transaction's own postcondition — every lane path in
  `HEAD`, the blob in `HEAD` equal to the store's current rendering, and that
  rendering equal to the planned post-state — and cleanup recomputes the same
  predicate rather than trusting `phase == discharged`.

That leaves `--resolution incomplete-integration`, which a caller *can* obtain by
assertion — and it purchases nothing. It lands no commit, creates no board-state
commit, prunes no workspace, never deletes the record, and makes `integrate`
refuse `story_integration_abandoned` on every Change Request revision for that
Story. It does release the lease, **explicitly and on the record**, because at a
terminal phase no further classification will read the write set and holding it
would freeze the Story's lane paths forever. What is prohibited is releasing the
lease silently as a side effect of deleting a record, and that route does not
exist.

**How an abandoned Story's board state still reaches trunk.** There is no
command that lands it. The route is ordinary: bring the board to the state the
transaction planned, commit those board paths yourself like any other change,
and then run `transaction discharge`. Discharge does not accept that as done —
it recomputes the postcondition from scratch: every planned lane path present in
`HEAD`, the blob in `HEAD` equal to the store's current rendering, and that
rendering equal to the planned post-state. Cleanup afterwards recomputes the
same predicate rather than trusting the recorded phase, so a discharge obtained
against a board that later drifts buys nothing either. The transaction record
survives the whole sequence; discharge retires it, it never erases it.

| Refusal | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `change_request_candidate_drift` | the worktree moved after the revision was published; rework and republish |
| `change_request_revision_conflict` | expected-revision CAS failed; re-read the current revision |
| `change_request_state_conflict` | the revision is not in a state that admits this transition |
| `change_request_evidence_missing` | the named acceptance evidence is not a task-scoped outcome resource, or is not one this reviewer run attached or updated |
| `change_request_checkpoint_conflict` | the Story branch moved under the checkpoint CAS |
| `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` | the checkpoint would also close the Story; use `worktree integrate`, which lands the branch first |
| `change_request_read_failed` | the record could not be read — this is never treated as "no Change Request" |
| `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized` | the caller is not the reviewer run that read this revision, or its identity could not be established |
| `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` | another task in this Story holds an unaccepted Change Request; review or revise that named task before starting this producer |
| `worktree_isolation_requires_local_board` | the resolved board is remote; Change Request construction and `accept_cr` both refuse before any side effect |

### Operating the workspace

```bash
task-board worktree status [STORY-ID]   # records, Change Requests, dropped board paths, lease
task-board worktree repair STORY-ID     # re-materialize a missing worktree from the recorded branch
task-board worktree checkpoint ELEMENT  # commit an accepted candidate and close the leaf
task-board worktree integrate STORY-ID --cr ELEMENT --revision N --commit-time RFC3339
task-board worktree transaction show STORY-ID            # phase and refusal of a stopped integration
task-board worktree abort STORY-ID      # stop using it; work is preserved
task-board worktree abort STORY-ID --discard --confirm   # destructive, explicit, two flags
task-board worktree gc                  # release leases whose holders are provably terminal
```

Nothing here forces. `repair` recreates a worktree only when the recorded
checkpoint is still reachable from the recorded branch; a rewritten branch is
`worktree_stale` and needs a human, because a "repair" that reset the branch
would destroy the work it claims to recover. `gc` reclaims leases and nothing
else, and only from a holder it can *prove* terminal — a run whose state cannot
be read is unknown, not finished.

**There is no `worktree cleanup` command.** A precondition-checked, non-forcing
cleanup — `git worktree remove` and `git branch -d` in their ordinary refusing
forms, parking at `cleanup_pending` on failure — is implemented in the worktree
lifecycle package, but **no production path calls it**, and a successful
`worktree integrate` records that safe cleanup is *eligible* rather than
performing it. So an integrated Story's workspace and branch are not reaped
automatically today. The two reachable routes are `worktree abort` (release the
Story, preserve everything) and `worktree abort --discard --confirm`, which is
the explicit destructive route and the only one that force-removes. Preferring
`abort` after integration is the safe default; the branch it preserves is
already squashed onto trunk.

Preserving `abort` releases the Story lease, which is an admission ticket for the
next writer, so it is coupled to proven termination of the current one:
`worktree_lease_unavailable` when the holder is live **or** cannot be classified.

A producer that dies with its checkout — an interrupted run, a cleaned `.temp`,
an unmounted volume — leaves Git holding the registration for a path that is no
longer there. That entry is not a workspace: `worktree status` reports the path
as `missing` and the tree as `registered but not checked out`, with Git's own
reason. The next producer spawn recreates the checkout from the recorded branch,
and `worktree repair` does the same on demand; both clear the leftover
registration with a plain, scoped `git worktree remove` first, which is allowed
exactly because there is nothing at the path to remove. If something *does*
occupy the path, both refuse with `worktree_path_collision` instead — recovery
never deletes what it did not create.

The repository probe distinguishes three answers, not two. Git exits non-zero
both when there is no repository and when it gives up before looking — an
unparsable config, an unreadable directory. Only Git's own outside-repository
answer is `worktree_not_a_git_repository`, which is the refusal that means "this
project does not use workspace isolation"; every other failure is
`worktree_git_command_failed` and carries what Git said. A broken Git is never
reported as an absent repository.
Cancel the holding run first — its terminal transition releases the lease — then
abort the workspace. Every terminal path releases it: operator cancel, queued
restart and reroute, autonomous recovery and its parking branch, supervision
deadline enforcement, provider-capability failure, and ordinary completion.

`status` distinguishes "Git says the worktree is gone" from "Git could not be
read". An unreadable `git worktree list` reports presence as `unknown`, never
`missing`, and blocks both cleanup and repair — `missing` is an operator's cue to
run `repair`, which would `git worktree add` over a checkout that may be present.

The filesystem read is separated the same way, and it is the read that decides
whether the checkout itself is there. `os.Lstat` fails both when nothing is at
the path and when the path could not be read at all, and only `ENOENT` and
`ENOTDIR` establish absence. A permission failure on a parent directory, an I/O
error, or an unmounted volume leaves presence `unknown`, reports the errno as
evidence, and refuses with `worktree_path_unreadable` rather than proceeding.
Nothing mutates Git or the filesystem on an unknown: provisioning refuses,
`repair` refuses, `cleanup` stays blocked, and `abort --discard --confirm` parks
at `cleanup_pending` without removing the worktree or deleting the branch.

Ref reads are separated the same way, and this is the read that decides whether
the managed Story branch is there. It is not a variation on the path rule: a ref
is answered by Git, not by `Lstat`, and Git's ordinary ref probes destroy the
distinction. `git show-ref --verify --quiet` exits `1` for a ref that is missing
**and** for one whose file could not be read; dropping `--quiet` exits `128` with
the identical "not a valid ref" message in both cases; and `git for-each-ref` is
worse than either, exiting `0` with empty output and downgrading the failure to
`warning: ignoring broken ref`. Only `git show-ref --exists` separates them —
`0` present, `2` missing, `1` or `128` a lookup that failed — so it is the probe
this package uses, and a Git older than 2.43 is answered `unknown` rather than
guessed at.

The consequence is concrete. An unreadable managed branch reported as a deleted
one let cleanup remove the checkout, skip the branch deletion, and persist a
terminal `cleaned` state for a branch it never observed — and once the checkout
is gone, that branch is the last durable copy of the Story's commits. So an
unreadable ref now blocks cleanup, parks `abort --discard --confirm` at
`cleanup_pending` without touching the checkout or the branch, refuses `repair`
under `worktree_ref_unreadable` instead of `worktree_missing`, and refuses
provisioning before any branch is created or worktree added. `worktree status`
reports the branch as `unknown`, never `missing`, because `missing` is the word
that sends an operator to recreate it.

Two derived reads follow the same rule. An ancestry probe that exits fatal leaves
checkpoint reachability `unknown` rather than reporting a branch rewritten past
its checkpoint, which is a data-loss narrative about history that is still
intact. And a base branch whose ref exists but whose commit cannot be read is
`worktree_ref_unreadable`, not `worktree_integration_base_missing`: the branch is
present, and telling an operator to create or fetch it is wrong.

The Story lease is read under the same rule, and it was the last one that was
not. The holder was projected as a plain run ID, so an empty value carried both
"this Story is free" and "the exclusion primitive could not be read" — and
cleanup blocked only on a *named* holder. A malformed or unreadable lease record
therefore arrived at the destructive path looking exactly like a free Story:
`cleanup` removed the checkout, deleted the branch, and persisted the terminal
`cleaned` state while a live producer may still have been writing there. A
diagnostic was already being emitted and changed nothing, because cleanup gates
on its blocker list rather than on its narration. The lease is now reported as a
decided/undecided pair: an unreadable lease blocks cleanup before any Git or
registry mutation, and `worktree status` prints `lease: unknown` rather than the
silence that a read, unheld lease prints — silence is what an operator reads as
"nobody is working here", which is the one conclusion an unread lease does not
support.

Trunk resolution's fallback chain is defined over *absences* — "no remote names
a HEAD, so use the current branch" — so a rule that could not be read stops the
resolution instead of deferring to the next one. When an unreadable remote HEAD
collapsed to "not set", the fallback fired on a failed read and the Story
workspace was branched off whatever the operator happened to have checked out,
with `current_branch` recorded as though that rule had been chosen. There is no
error in that failure mode, which is why it is guarded explicitly. Discovery
itself has since become remote-neutral — it walks every configured remote's
HEAD rather than one named `origin` — but the same unreadable-versus-absent
rule still governs it: a remote whose HEAD or tracking cannot be read stops
resolution rather than being silently skipped as though it had no HEAD at all.

Git's own `prunable` annotation is not independent corroboration here, and this
is observed rather than assumed. Git derives it from its own stat of the recorded
gitdir, which fails exactly as ours does: a live checkout behind a directory with
no traversal permission is listed `prunable gitdir file points to non-existent
location`. The annotation is authoritative about a path Git could read, and about
a path neither can read it is the same fabricated absence in Git's voice — so it
is consulted only once presence is known.

### Refusals

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `worktree_isolation_requires_local_board` | The resolved board is remote. Every managed-workspace surface refuses before any side effect. |
| `worktree_not_a_git_repository` | The control root is not inside a Git working tree, so a repository-mutating producer has nothing to isolate in. |
| `worktree_trunk_ambiguous` | No configured base branch, and remote-neutral discovery (every remote's HEAD plus matching tracking config) does not land on exactly one branch, or a no-remote repository has detached `HEAD`. Set `integration_base_branch` explicitly, or repair remote HEAD/tracking so exactly one branch qualifies. |
| `worktree_integration_base_missing` | The named base branch does not exist. Create or fetch it. Reported only from a ref lookup that positively answered "missing"; a lookup that failed is `worktree_ref_unreadable`. |
| `worktree_trunk_authority_unresolved` | A remote HEAD, branch tracking configuration, or upstream ref could not be read, or tracking config is partial, malformed, or internally inconsistent. Unknown, and never treated as "no upstream configured". |
| `worktree_trunk_authority_refresh_failed` | The configured upstream could not be fetched, or the freshly fetched ref could not be resolved. Restore access to the upstream and fetch ref, then retry. |
| `worktree_trunk_local_ahead_of_authority` | The local integration branch has commits the freshly fetched upstream does not — unpublished local work. Push or otherwise publish them, then retry. |
| `worktree_trunk_diverged_from_authority` | The local integration branch and its freshly fetched upstream have diverged; neither is an ancestor of the other. Reconcile the local branch with upstream, then retry. |
| `worktree_branch_collision` | `task-board/story/<STORY-ID>` exists but is not a workspace task-board owns. |
| `worktree_path_collision` | The managed path is occupied by something task-board did not create. |
| `worktree_ownership_mismatch` | A workspace record names a different repository, Story, branch, path, or client. |
| `worktree_lease_unavailable` | Another run holds the Story lease and is live or unclassifiable. Ordinarily a scheduling condition; on `abort` and `repair` it is a refusal to race a writer. |
| `worktree_git_command_failed` | A Git read this decision depends on failed, so the state is unknown rather than absent. Repair refuses; cleanup stays blocked. |
| `worktree_path_unreadable` | The managed path could not be read (permissions, I/O, an unmounted volume), so its presence is unknown rather than absent. Carries the errno. Nothing is created, cleared, or removed on it. |
| `worktree_ref_unreadable` | A Git ref lookup failed, so the ref's existence is unknown rather than missing — an unreadable loose ref or `packed-refs`, an unreadable base commit, an ancestry probe that exited fatal, or a Git too old for `show-ref --exists`. Carries Git's own diagnostic. Provisioning, `repair`, `cleanup`, and `abort --discard` all refuse on it; nothing is branched, added, removed, or attested. |
| `worktree_missing` / `worktree_stale` | The worktree or branch is gone, or the branch no longer contains the recorded checkpoint. |
| `worktree_cleanup_blocked` | A cleanup precondition is unmet; the workspace is retained. Includes a Story lease that could not be read: unknown blocks, because "I could not tell whether anyone is writing here" only supports assuming someone is. |
| `worktree_abort_requires_confirmation` | `--discard` without `--confirm`. |

Every one of these fires before assignment, prompt composition, RUN creation, or
any board mutation, so a refused spawn leaves the element exactly as it was.

### Known gaps in the shipped surface

Recorded here rather than left for an operator to hit, because each one is a
place where the design's intent and the current binary differ:

| Gap | Effect today | Tracked as |
|---|---|---|
| The safe `Cleanup` path has no production caller and there is no `worktree cleanup` command | an integrated Story's workspace and branch are not reaped automatically; use `worktree abort`, or `abort --discard --confirm` to destroy | — |
| `board_shared_plane_delete` has no reachable production input | deleting an ancestor `progress.md` makes the loader lose that whole subtree, so integration refuses earlier with `integration_indeterminate`; the store cannot yet tell a deleted file from an unreadable subtree | `TASK-260807-2seteb` |
| The `board_delta_moved` message says `worktree status` reports `integrated_board_uncommitted` | no command prints that string; `worktree transaction show` is the surface that reports the transaction | — |
| `accept_cr` is registered on the mutation path only | `task-board m 'accept_cr(...)'` works, but `task-board q 'schema(mutation=accept_cr)'` answers `unknown mutation`, so the usual on-error schema discovery does not find it. Its signature is `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=NAME)` | — |

Two gaps outside this surface matter to anyone following this reference today:
a suppressed provider group clears only by ladder expiry, because the
success-observation path has no production caller (`BUG-260807-3hd3uz`), and
`task-board claude` cannot start a session at all on Claude Code 2.1.223
(`BUG-260807-1z9tid`).

## Board-Managed Selection Procedure

Choose every spawn for **maximum task quality per token**, not by reflexively
using the strongest model or highest supported effort.

When `spawn.workload_classes` is configured, this procedure is mandatory:
query the workload-aware target preflight, reason from its available pairs and
provenance, then explicitly spawn with the same class transport, fresh snapshot
digest, and a one-line recommendation rationale. The selected available pair
may follow or consciously reject the ordered recommendations. Never translate
rank into authority, a default, or an automatic widening.

1. Assess the task's scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation
   burden, and expected context and token cost (prompt, source material,
   iterations, and likely retries).
2. Query the registry and select the strongest suitable explicit model/effort
   pair that can credibly pass the existing quality gates.
3. For models that support it, use **`max` as the highest recommended
   board-managed effort**. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible, but enables
   model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning.
4. Prefer task-board roles, tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, dependencies, and
   reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. That keeps work
   observable instead of duplicating delegation inside a model call.
5. Use a lower-cost pair only when bounded scope can still meet the same
   required test, validation, outcome, and review gates.

Choose the role from the deliverable, not from the provider or model:

| Task type | Role |
| --- | --- |
| Research, analysis, or investigation | `researcher` |
| Code implementation, bug fixes, or refactoring | `developer` |
| Test implementation or validation | `tester` |
| Genuine system-design tradeoff or unresolved cross-module contract | `solution-architect` |
| Documentation | `doc-writer` |
| Code, documentation, or acceptance review | `reviewer` |
| Board decomposition, routing, and delivery supervision | `orchestrator` (`coordinator` is the legacy alias) |

The current registry remains the executable source of truth. The table below
preserves the human selection tiers and model-specific effort caps; query
`models(agent=<selected-agent>)` before launch for the live registered set.

| Work tier | Agent | Model | Recommended effort | Highest supported effort |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical | Codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | `max` | `ultra` |
| Balanced agentic work | Codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | `max` | `ultra` |
| Fast, affordable agentic work | Codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | `max` | `max` (no `ultra`) |
| Complex coding and research | Codex | `gpt-5.5` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` (no `max`/`ultra`) |
| Everyday coding | Codex | `gpt-5.4` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` |
| Simpler cost-sensitive work | Codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` |
| Ultra-fast code iteration | Codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` |
| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | Claude | `claude-fable-5` | `high` | `max` |
| Everyday complex Claude work (recommended) | Claude | `claude-opus-5` | `high` | `max` |
| Previous-generation everyday Claude work | Claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | `xhigh` | `max` |
| Routine Claude work | Claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | `high` | `max` |
| Fast, bounded Claude checks | Claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | none | none; Haiku takes no reasoning effort |
| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | Qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | `xhigh` | `max` |
| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | Qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | `xhigh` | `max` |

Claude's own effort vocabulary is `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max` — no
`ultra`, which is Codex-only. Legacy `claude-opus-4-6` and
`claude-sonnet-4-6` predate `xhigh` and cap at `max`; both current and legacy
Haiku IDs take no reasoning effort at all. The Claude capability `PolicyRank`
ladder is `claude-fable-5` (80), `claude-opus-5` (75), legacy
`claude-opus-4-8` (70), `claude-sonnet-5` (60), legacy
`claude-opus-4-6` (30), legacy `claude-sonnet-4-6` (20), and
`claude-haiku-4-5` (10); its dated snapshot shares rank 10.
Rank 40 is intentionally open for a future capability insertion, and so is
rank 50. Grandfathered v2
ordered and effort-only admission comes from the
immutable rollout snapshot, not this mutable rank ladder. Legacy IDs remain
spawnable for compatibility but are not normal new-work picks.

V3 admission is a separate explicit policy: only the target role's admitted
`(model, effort)` pair set — `admitted_pairs` — is authoritative. Operators
declare that pair set via `allowed_models` or `entries` (see
[Ceiling entries](task-board-config.md#ceiling-entries)); only its configured
`resolution_model` may replace a miss.
Capability `PolicyRank` remains truthful evidence and may be re-ranked without
changing either the v2 snapshot or any v3 set. Use target-only `project_config`
for the spawn decision; use the full v2 migration projection only for an
explicit, reviewed, repository-local edit.

**Budget is unlimited by default.** Do not pass `--budget` unless the user
explicitly asks to cap spending. A cap that stops a child halfway usually costs
more after the retry; unlimited is cheaper than retries.

For prompt-mode Claude spawns, `--budget` maps to Claude's provider-enforced
print-mode cap. A manager-owned goal-bound Claude session uses a persistent PTY,
where that provider flag is invalid; the requested amount is retained in run
audit state but is advisory and not a hard cap. Do not promise a hard USD limit
for that path until manager-side accounting exists.

For example, `ultra` remains a valid explicit compatibility selection when a
caller deliberately wants model-internal delegation, but it is not the normal
recommendation for board work:

```bash
# Explicit compatibility choice, not normal board guidance
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort ultra
```

Use `task-board codex` or `task-board claude` for a manager-owned primary
session. Goal-bound owners use the same manager regardless of which wrapper
initiated the spawn. Codex additionally supports in-session delivery of tracked
terminal completions:

```bash
task-board codex
```

If that live Codex session should auto-nudge safe `waitingOnUserInput` stalls,
enable the Codex-only anti-stall policy either in
`task-board.config.json -> session_manager.providers.codex.auto_continue` or
explicitly per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue ...`. Explicit
`--auto-continue*` flags override config values.

## Fresh-Session Delegation Smoke

Use this smoke whenever the skill or coordinator role changes and you need to
verify that a fresh session chooses tracked child runs instead of doing board
task work inline.

Expected behavior:
- the fresh session loads the `project-management` skill and treats itself as
  the coordinator unless it is already inside a spawned role prompt
- it runs target-only `project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER?)` before spawn decisions
- it creates or selects real board items with description, AC, and checklist DoD
- executable work is delegated with explicit role/model/reasoning selection
- review is also delegated with explicit `--role reviewer`, model, and reasoning after the producer
  reaches `to-review`

Minimal operator prompt:

```text
Use the project-management skill. In this repo, solve a tiny safe board-backed
task end to end using the skill's normal workflow. The task should require one
producer role and one reviewer role, and it must leave an outcome resource with
the run evidence.
```

Pass signals:
- at least one `RUN-...` appears under `.temp/spawn-runs/`
- `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'` shows role-shaped assignees such as
  `[implementer] developer (codex)` or `[reviewer] reviewer (codex)`
- fresh-session logs include a fully explicit command such as
  `task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`
  before any specialist deliverable is produced inline

Fail signals:
- the fresh session assigns tasks and then writes code, docs, research, tests, or
  review findings itself without a `RUN-...`
- it performs reviewer work inline after a producer reaches `to-review`
- it treats `task-board spawn` as optional ceremony instead of the execution
  path for board tasks

When the smoke fails, save the fresh-session log and board state as an outcome
resource on the tracking task/bug before patching the skill again.

```bash
# Highest recommended board-managed Codex spawn with explicit selection
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max

# Validate instruction resource is linked before spawning
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md

# Explicit Claude budget only when the user requested a spending cap
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --budget 5.0

# Balanced Codex agentic work
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max

# Fast Codex agentic work
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max

# Context plane (only when features.context_sharing is on)
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --context CTX-260728-a1b2c3
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --no-context
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --register-context task

# Inspect and synchronize on the tracked run later
task-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn observe RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:0 --until terminal --format compact
```

**Model validation:** Missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs block before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit models remain forward-compatible with a warning. Inspect the current compatibility matrix through local `models(agent=<selected-agent>)` even when the board itself is remote. Qwen model discovery also reports the Qwen Code context window and Token Plan Team seat pricing/monthly-quota applicability. Qwen ordinary children share the tracked prompt/process/run lifecycle, use a stream-JSON initialize frame to carry effort, and do not support goal-bound owner launches.

**Auto-detection:** Codex runtimes expose `CODEX_SESSION` or `CODEX_THREAD_ID` (and sometimes `CODEX_CI` in managed environments). If any of these are present, affinity is Codex; otherwise it is Claude. These hints are agent-affinity signals only: they do not discover an endpoint, authenticate a thread, create an addressable caller goal plane, or select Qwen. Canonical exclusive/mixed policy and active schedule windows constrain affinity before use. Auto-detection never chooses a model or reasoning effort. Use an in-set `--agent` to make provider selection explicit.

**Prompt composition is board-centric:**
- task description comes from the element README
- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element
- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD
- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts

Normal spawn does **not** require temp task briefs or temp DoD files. Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they are not the primary workflow and they do not replace board data as the source of truth.

Before launch, spawn validates that the board task brief is actually usable:
- description must be real, not empty / placeholder / one-line fluff
- acceptance criteria must be real, not empty / placeholder / one-line fluff
- task-specific DoD must exist on the board checklist

If these checks fail, spawn should return a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.

**Safety flag:** `--instruction NAME` validates that a precondition resource matching NAME is linked to the task before spawning. If not found, returns an error with exact commands to link and retry.

## Close the Review Loop

The Orchestrator owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`:

1. A producer's `to-review` handoff triggers an explicitly selected reviewer child immediately.
2. Accepted work reaches `done` from the reviewer verdict.
3. Requested changes become preserved rework context for the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`.
4. Revised work goes through a new reviewer. Repeat until accepted.
5. A child that exits before handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` is inspected, retried, or rerouted through a focused child; recoverable work is never handed to the human.
6. A genuine `blocked` verdict stops the loop only for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, with the full evidence packet and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.

Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are not a reason to stop or ask the human to review. Before genuine escalation, persist the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.

## Preflight And Audit Hygiene

Spawn-contract reviews get noisy if you search the whole repo without filtering transient runtime copies.

- Use target-only `project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER?)` for an actual spawn decision. Use full `project_config()` only when auditing local/remote runtime configuration.
- Exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide grep/review by default. This repo contains historical task-scoped worktrees such as `.temp/*/skill-project-management-worktree/` that can preserve pre-refactor spawn files and create false drift.
- Prefer searching canonical source paths directly: `README.md`, `SKILL.md`, `references/`, `.specs/` (and, in source checkouts only, the CLI module sources — installed skills exclude build roots, use the `task-board` shim instead).
- If you need runtime artifacts, inspect the specific run directory under `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../` instead of grepping all of `.temp/`.

Recommended search pattern:

```bash
rg -n "spawn|project_config|--background|task-path|dod-path" \
  README.md SKILL.md references .specs \
  -g '!**/.temp/**'
# In source checkouts, add the CLI module sources (e.g. tools/board-cli) manually — they are build roots excluded from installed skill context.
```

**What spawn does:**
1. Validates element on board
2. Loads role config from `.roles/<name>/role.md` (required)
3. Loads task metadata from the board (README fields, checklist, parent, resources)
4. Verifies the task brief is usable (description + AC)
5. Verifies task-specific DoD exists on the board
6. Composes prompt from role template plus board state
7. Resolves and validates the selected provider's project MCP launch composition, or records a bare-launch degradation
8. Assigns to agent (name includes archetype: `[implementer] developer (codex)`)
9. Adds any missing role baseline DoD items to the board checklist without duplicating already injected items
10. Saves prompt to `.temp/prompts/{YYMMDD}-{HH-mm-ss}_{agent-name}_{task-id}.md`
11. Creates `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../{manifest,state,runner.log}`
12. Queues a tracked background runner and returns a `RUN-...` handle immediately
13. The runner claims a slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the agent process, streams logwork, and runs the completion hook
14. Logs queue/start/completion and sanitized launch-composition details to element notes

---

## Wait And Completion Mechanics

`task-board spawn wait RUN-...` does **not** wait on a shell background job and does **not** try to keep track of the child agent PID from the original `task-board spawn` caller.

This section summarizes the runtime behavior. The durable source of truth for completion semantics is [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](../.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).

Instead, the contract is:

1. `task-board spawn` writes `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../{manifest.json,state.json}`
2. Background `spawn-runner` process starts and moves the run from `queued` to `running`
3. That runner launches the real child agent and blocks on it directly
4. When the child agent exits, the runner executes the completion hook
5. The completion hook syncs the spawn-log resource, appends completion notes, and applies any post-run artifact/status checks
6. Only after that does the runner write terminal run state (`completed` or `failed`) back to `state.json`
7. `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` polls `state.json` until it sees a terminal status

This is why coordinators should treat `RUN-...` as the handle for orchestration barriers. The shell process that originally invoked `task-board spawn` is intentionally not the source of truth once the tracked background runner has been registered.

The runner also records its PID and updates a heartbeat while it is alive, including while queued or paused. Wait/follow observers run PID-aware stale repair: a live PID survives delayed heartbeats, while dead `running`, `queued`, or `paused` runners become failed instead of hanging observers forever.

### Bounds on a running child

Two independent bounds can end a run the orchestrator is not watching. Both live
in the product, because the failure they prevent is precisely an orchestrator
that does not notice.

- **`--timeout`** bounds one launch. It is a real deadline on the ordinary
  prompt-mode path as well as the managed goal paths: at expiry the launcher
  sends SIGTERM, waits 30 s, then SIGKILL, and reports a non-recoverable
  runtime failure so one deadline does not become a successor chain retrying
  the same wall clock. Omitting it leaves the launch unbounded, which is what
  an unconfigured repository still gets.
- **Supervision** bounds the orchestrator's obligation across the whole run,
  in minutes, per provider and role. It samples only free signals — local run
  state, child pid liveness, and this run's own event log — and never attempts
  a direction check, because mid-run content is not observable on any board
  surface. See [supervision (minutes)](task-board-config.md#supervision-minutes)
  for the schema, what counts as child-authored progress, the `on_deadline`
  table, and the named limitations.

`task-board spawn status RUN-...` prints `Supervision Breach` and, when the
deadline left a child running, the handle for it: `Orphaned Child PID` for a
prompt-mode child, or `Orphaned Child Session` for a goal-bound or
context-bound owner, which runs inside a Session Manager session and has no pid.
Under `on_deadline: park` that is the deliberate outcome; under
`kill_and_park`/`cancel` it means the termination did not succeed, and the note
on the element says so rather than claiming a kill. What a successful
termination claims is the process *group* that was signalled — `child terminated
(process group 4821 confirmed gone)` — because that is the identity the signals
addressed and the only one the confirmation can prove. The leader pid is not a
usable answer here: a provider wrapper exits the moment it forks, so its death
says nothing about the worker still running in the group beside it.

Both launch shapes are enforced, and both distinguish the fence from the fate of
a child already running. Under `kill_and_park`/`cancel` the ordinary launcher
signals the child's process group and a managed owner is stopped through its
session; under `park` the ordinary launcher stops waiting without signalling and
the managed launcher stops waiting without cancelling, so the run releases its
slot while the child keeps working. A deadline that finds neither handle bounds
the run anyway but reports that nothing was terminated — an unstoppable child is
stated, never assumed dead.

**Checking on a run does not extend its rope.** An eligible event class is not
evidence of authorship, because `directive_acknowledged`, `goal_created` and
`goal_revised` are all reachable from an operator's shell:
`task-board spawn directive RUN-... request_progress` marks the operator's own
directive acknowledged the moment it is stored, before the child has polled
anything, and `task-board spawn goal upsert RUN-...` run without
`TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` activates a revision as an operator. Those classes count
only when the event carries proof that the OWNING child authored it — the
acting process carried this run's `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`. A sibling child's actor,
a system actor, and an event with no recorded author are all foreign. Otherwise
a supervisor checking on a stalled run would keep it alive, which is the
feature inverted.

**What that guarantees, and what it does not.** The guarantee is against
*accident*: no ordinary operator action extends any run's deadline, because the
runtime issues `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` to spawned children and to nothing else. It
is *not* a guarantee against deliberate impersonation. RUN ids appear on the
board, and an operator on the same machine who exports one can make a write
count as the child's. Adding a token would not change that — the same operator
already owns the board files, the `.temp/spawn-runs/` state directory and the
process table. The limitation is accepted and bounded by the same two mitigations
as a child that writes real changes while making no headway: the optional
absolute `max_runtime_minutes`, and the successor chain bound.

### Fused Spawn And Wait (`spawn ... --wait`)

`--wait` fuses the two-step `spawn` + `spawn wait` flow into one CLI invocation. The two-step flow remains fully supported; fused `--wait` is a convenience for the single-child / single-barrier case, not a replacement.

```bash
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --wait
```

Contract:

- `--background` is still required. `--wait` changes observation, not execution placement: the child agent still runs under the tracked background runner, and the run is registered identically to the two-step flow — same `RUN-...` handle, manifest/state/events under `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`, directive plane, completion hook, and observation hints.
- After queueing, the same in-process wait loop as standalone `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` takes over (they share one code path, so polling, terminal reporting, and exit semantics cannot drift). The command blocks until the run reaches a terminal state.
- Exit code matches standalone `spawn wait`: success only when the run reaches `completed`; `failed`, `cancelled`, and any other non-completed terminal state return a non-zero error exit.
- The fused wait has no wait-side timeout; it blocks until terminal state. `spawn --timeout` remains the child agent process timeout. Use standalone `spawn wait RUN-... --timeout 5m` when you need a bounded barrier.
- Text output prints the queued `RUN-...` and observation commands first (marked "Fused wait active: no separate spawn wait invocation is needed"), then the terminal report. In `--json` mode the command waits first and emits a single response whose `runStatus`/`exitCode` reflect the terminal state, with the usual `observation.*` contract included.

Claude-Code orchestrator example — ONE Bash tool call, wrapped by the caller in `run_in_background: true`:

```bash
# One harness Bash call (run_in_background: true) — registers the tracked run,
# blocks in-process to terminal state, and its exit wakes the Claude session:
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --wait
```

**Hard boundary — `run_in_background` stays caller/harness-side.** Whether that single `spawn ... --wait` invocation blocks the orchestrator session or runs as a harness background task is decided by the caller (the Claude Code `Bash` tool's `run_in_background` parameter), and only by the caller. There is no API through which the CLI or server could place itself into a harness background slot, so task-board cannot emulate or move that decision CLI/server-side. This is a structural boundary of the harness model, not a bug or missing feature. The fused flag only reduces two CLI calls to one; harness placement remains the orchestrator's decision at every callsite.

When to prefer which flow:

- **Fused `--wait`**: exactly one child and one barrier — a single sequenced producer step, or a Claude orchestrator that wants one background Bash call per child instead of two.
- **Two-step `spawn` + `spawn wait`**: fan-out of several independent children first, then one background wait per `RUN-...`; bounded waits (`--timeout`); or waiting from a different process/session than the one that spawned.

---

## Orchestrator Monitoring Patterns

`task-board spawn` is background execution with tracking. Do not wrap it in shell backgrounding (`&`, `nohup`, runtime `run_in_background`) just to avoid blocking; the command already returns once the tracked runner is registered.

### Pattern A: Fan Out Tracked Background Runs

```bash
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
task-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
task-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role tester --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
```

Each call returns a `RUN-...` identifier plus a task- and run-scoped logwork path. Concurrent runs for the same task and role therefore keep separate log files and outcome resources. The runner enforces shared concurrency from `task-board.config.json -> spawn.max_parallel` (default `20`), so you can queue more runs than the machine is allowed to execute at once.

### Pattern B: Replay And Follow Event Cursors

```bash
task-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
task-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
task-board spawn observe RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5 --until terminal --format compact
task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn directives RUN-260320-abc123
task-board spawn directive RUN-260320-abc123 cancel
task-board spawn goal RUN-260320-abc123
```

Every valid explicit spawn prints these monitor commands automatically after queueing, seeded with cursor `RUN-ID:0` and bound to the same `task-board` executable / board context that launched the run.
In JSON mode the same contract is returned under `observation`:
`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, and `inSessionDelivery`.
`observation.runtime` reports the orchestrator session runtime (`codex-session`, `claude-session`, or `external`) and `observation.delivery` names its non-blocking completion channel (`codex-in-session`, `claude-background-wait`, or `external-watch`).

Use the printed `observe --until terminal --format compact` command as the
default single surface: it consumes events newer than its durable cursor and
appends terminal checklist/outcome handoff evidence without replaying earlier
history. Use `status` for a diagnostic snapshot, `events` for replay, `watch`
for follow mode, and `wait` only for a barrier that does not need event/handoff
detail. Do not stack these reads on the routine success path.
In JSON mode, `spawn watch` uses notification NDJSON framing only (one notification object per line, no trailing summary payload).

### Pattern C: Durable Directive Plane

- `spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` records a durable operator directive with acknowledgement state.
- `spawn directives RUN-...` replays the directive history for that run.
- `cancel` is applied immediately for queued runs and terminates the background runner/process group for running runs when the platform supports it.
- `pause_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, and `nudge` are cooperative signals. Current runtimes expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`; the child may poll `task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"` at safe checkpoints.
- `restart` and `reroute_after_checkpoint` create successor `RUN-...` records instead of rewriting existing run history. Running reroute/pause requests are rejected when the runtime cannot checkpoint safely in place.
- Both directive commands print the current active goal header before directive details. New records persist the immutable goal ID/revision under which they were issued; legacy records print `issued_under_goal: unknown`.
- Restart and a reroute that preserves the current goal ID, revision, role binding, scope, kind, predicate, and intent remain owner-agent recovery actions. Goal-bound `cancel` and non-monotonic reroute are operator-authoritative controls and require a non-empty reason. A structured replacement validates its requested scope/kind/predicate against the successor role before it writes the directive or successor.

### Pattern D: Goal-Bound Delivery Pool

After plan approval and before the first producer launch or delivery status transition, start one independently tracked orchestrator owner:

```bash
task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth \
  --role orchestrator \
  --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b \
  --background \
  --agent codex \
  --model gpt-5.6-sol \
  --reasoning-effort max
```

The CLI resolves containers to an active dependency-closed task/bug pool,
derives `delivery_pool/accepted_done` from the normalized orchestrator role,
reserves the RUN/GOAL lineage, validates provider capability and the
primary-session launch plan, and only then permits normal launch side effects.
The Session Manager attaches the exact `run/RUN-*` owner, reconciles its native
goal, and submits the assignment only after the session's applied revision
matches the authoritative v2 head. Nested specialists receive role-derived
`role_handoff` or `reviewer_verdict` owners without replacing the parent
`delivery_pool` condition.

The authoritative state is the `board-goal-v2` `run/RUN-*` owner head and its
immutable revision history. `manifest.launch_goal`, `state.active_goal_ref`,
and run-local revision files are one-release `board-goal-v1` compatibility
mirrors written with the v2 promotion. Failed native activation leaves the
prior head active; an unbound head is `pending-unbound` until the provider
acknowledges that exact revision.

Parent and run owner heads are isolated manager slots. The spawned provider
environment strips parent manager/app-server endpoints, auth indirections and
their token values, and parent provider session markers. Persisted caller-plane
fields are decode-only compatibility data; new launches issue no caller RPC.
One-release restoration remains only for an active legacy manifest that
already contains an exact caller snapshot.

At every checkpoint:

```bash
task-board spawn goal "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
```

If a nested target resolves outside the current pool, `spawn` returns non-zero `goal_scope_mismatch` before assignment, prompt, manifest, provider, or child-process effects. The payload classifies missing leaves as `adjacent`, `outside`, or `mixed` and includes the exact optimistic update command. Run that command only when the work is genuinely required, then retry the spawn.

Nested spawn also fails closed on its declared parent execution context. While
a legacy already-running Claude owner has `successor_required`, the command
returns typed `goal_provider_binding_pending` with `side_effects=false`; new
goal-bound Claude owners start directly in manager-held PTYs. If the declared
parent manifest, state, or active revision cannot be loaded, the command
returns `goal_parent_context_unavailable` rather than treating the caller as
non-goal.

Owner-agent changes are monotonic: create with `--scope`, expand with `--add-scope`, refresh only the non-authoritative intent, and supply `--if-revision` for updates. Upsert responses expose `mutation.previous_revision` as `0` on creation, the prior active revision on a new immutable revision, and the current revision on an idempotent replay. Restart and same-semantics reroute retain that latest revision under a rebound successor owner. Scope removal, structural replacement, weakening, kind changes, and cancellation cross the operator directive boundary and create durable cancelled/superseded lineage rather than successful delivery.

For a legacy already-running Claude run, `successor_required` retains its
one-release safe-checkpoint behavior. New manager-owned sessions do not use
that split path.

Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch or transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet producer handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer branches, and unmet delivery acceptance persist a structured recovery cause and automatically queue a successor. A goal-bound predecessor rebinds the same active goal revision; a context-only predecessor drops its predecessor-owned writable child and managed envelope; a run owned by no plane gets an ordinary prompt-mode successor. The predecessor is superseded by recovery, never successful delivery.

Rate/usage-limit recovery preserves the real provider error on the run and
element, but never parses or trusts a reset timestamp in that text. The runtime
uses bounded local exponential backoff, then probes availability by launching
the same persisted model. Recovery lineage is capped at three successors; when
the cap is reached, the last run becomes terminal `recovery_parked` and the
element returns to its actionable queue (`to-review` for review work, `to-dev`
for development) with an operator-action note instead of remaining silently
parked in an active status. A non-recoverable provider-capability failure does
not create a successor, but follows the same board-visible treatment: its real
provider cause is recorded on the terminal run and element, and the element is
routed back to the actionable role queue. A provider owner-session binding
rotation preserves the existing recovery lineage and does not spend one of
those attempts.

Failed terminalization uses a durable element-routing outbox. The run records
the exact operator message and pending routing before it becomes terminal. If
the board write fails, status/wait/follow repair retries the same routing after
the board recovers and records a routed timestamp only after both the note and
role-specific status mutation succeed. Note delivery is acknowledged separately,
so retrying a failed status mutation does not append the same operator note
again. This covers recovery preparation,
attempt-cap parking, provider capability, heartbeat expiry, and generic failed
terminal paths. Repair isolates failures per run: one deleted or otherwise
unroutable element cannot abort the rest of the sweep, block slot claims, or
break waits for unrelated runs. Runtime-owned exponential backoff spaces
routing attempts. Transient board failures stay pending without consuming the
terminal bound. Permanence requires structured local `board.ErrNotFound` or a
typed remote mutation failure decoded from the server's HTTP 200 `ok:false`
envelope with the exact `element <ID>: not found` message shape. Remote 404/5xx
responses and incidental "not found" prose remain retryable. Three consecutive
permanent failures exhaust, and that state is preserved on run status with the
exact last error.
Operator cancellation stages the same outbox with the supplied reason and
returns the element to its role-specific actionable queue.

Stale-run repair follows that same no-silence rule. When a tracked runner dies
and its heartbeat expires, the run terminates with `heartbeat_expired`; the
element records the exact cause and returns from the run's active start status
to `to-review` for reviewer work or `to-dev` for development. This path parks
for explicit orchestration rather than guessing whether a killed runner is safe
to resume.

If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, its manifest and state
remain non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start cause, retry time,
and scheduler lease. Status, wait, and follow observers claim an eligible or
expired lease under the scheduler lock, retry that same successor outside the
lock, and clear the deferred marker only after runner start/heartbeat. The
predecessor may already be superseded, but the same immutable goal revision
still has exactly one retryable owner instead of two terminal runs.

Provider `blocked` and a role that exits at board `blocked` use the same
Stop-The-Line gate. Board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped evidence
packet terminates the run with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; reviewer runs
also persist the `stop_the_line` verdict. This branch never satisfies delivery
acceptance. Without the status/evidence pair, the boundary claim is recoverable
and the same revision is queued again.

Producer provider exit is not handoff success. A `role_handoff` run must reach its immutable role end status and add or update a task-scoped outcome artifact relative to its launch snapshot. Zero exit while the board remains in `analysis`/`development`, or at the handoff status without evidence, queues autonomous same-revision recovery.

After attaching the task-scoped outcome, producers should use
`task-board handoff TASK-ID --role ROLE`. The command resolves the configured
role end status, verifies every checklist item and an actual stored task-scoped
outcome, then performs the evidence decision and ordinary status transition
under one mutation lock. It reports all missing checklist/outcome evidence and
leaves status unchanged when the gate fails. Spawn-log artifacts do not count as
producer outcomes, and the runtime still enforces its launch-relative outcome
snapshot independently.

A tracked producer's direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)` for its assigned
element is refused with remediation to use this command. This is a spawned-role
workflow guardrail, not a new authorization boundary for ordinary operators.

Delivery success is not provider completion alone. Reviewer goals persist exactly one evidence-backed branch derived from board state: `accepted`, `changes_requested`, or `stop_the_line`; an exit while still `reviewing` triggers recovery. `required`/`light` leaves need board `done`, task-scoped producer outcome evidence, and a later reviewer whose persisted branch is `accepted`. `none` leaves need `done`, producer outcome evidence, and the owner’s completion-time evidence check.

### Pattern E: Delivery Boundary (Watcher vs In-Session)

- `spawn events`, `spawn watch`, and `spawn observe` are external watcher surfaces built on persisted run events.
- They can notify a human/operator and feed TUI/remote push adapters.
- They do **not** inject inbound text into the currently active orchestrator session by default.
- When the orchestrator session is launched through `task-board codex`, tracked terminal state is also injected back into the active Codex thread through the app-server runtime adapter.
- `task-board codex` may also auto-steer safe `waitingOnUserInput` pauses inside that active thread when `session_manager.providers.codex.auto_continue.enabled=true` or `--auto-continue` is set for the launch.
- The v1 anti-stall controller never auto-continues `waitingOnApproval` and does not try to answer child-process terminal prompts on behalf of the operator.
- If runtime-native direct push is unavailable, use terminal delta observation (`spawn observe --cursor ... --until terminal --format compact`) as the default; replay (`spawn events --cursor ...`) and snapshot checks (`spawn status`) remain diagnostic alternatives.

These are observation/session enhancements only. They do not determine whether
either primary launcher can create a goal-bound tracked owner.

### Pattern F: Claude Orchestrator Non-Blocking Fan-Out

Claude Code has no injection API for a live session, so there is no Claude
counterpart of the Codex app-server push adapter. The equivalent non-blocking
channel is the harness itself: Claude Code re-invokes the session when a
tracked background command exits. Spawn detects a Claude session through the
`CLAUDECODE` environment variable and reports
`observation.delivery = claude-background-wait`.

The orchestrator pattern:

1. Spawn all independent children first — each fully selected tracked spawn returns its `RUN-...` immediately.
2. For every returned run, start the printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` command as a **harness background task** (Claude Code `Bash` with `run_in_background`), not as a blocking foreground call.
3. Keep orchestrating: the session stays free while children execute; each background wait exits when its run reaches terminal state, and the harness wakes the session with that result.
4. On each wake-up, inspect `spawn status RUN-...` / outcome resources and route the next step (reviewer spawn, rework, dependent task) — then return to waiting on the remaining runs.

```bash
# One Claude orchestrator, five concurrent children, zero blocking:
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5   # -> RUN-A
task-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5   # -> RUN-B
# ... spawn remaining children ...
# then one harness background task per run:
#   run_in_background: task-board spawn wait RUN-A
#   run_in_background: task-board spawn wait RUN-B
```

Do not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` itself in a background helper — `--background`
already returns a tracked run. Only the `spawn wait` observation barrier goes
into the harness background slot. Sequenced work (producer -> reviewer) still
uses an explicit `spawn wait` barrier before spawning the dependent child.

Single-child variant: the fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` collapses
steps 1–2 into ONE harness background Bash call per child — it registers the
tracked run identically and then blocks in-process to terminal state, so its
exit wakes the session exactly like a standalone background `spawn wait` (see
"Fused Spawn And Wait" above). Wrapping that single call in `run_in_background`
remains the caller/harness decision — task-board cannot make it.

### Removed: Launch-Time Foreground Mode

The old launch-time foreground/attached harness (the original foreground `task-board spawn TASK --wait`, hidden `--attached`, and proposed `--foreground`) has been removed from the public spawn path. It mixed execution placement with observation and made the parent/orchestrator act like a worker.

The current `--wait` flag is not that mode returning: it is a fused observation barrier on top of the unchanged tracked background runner (see "Fused Spawn And Wait" above). Execution placement stays background-only — `--background` is required with or without `--wait`.

Use tracked background spawn first, then wait on the `RUN-...` handle when a blocking barrier is needed:

```bash
task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
```

The postmortem for that removal is attached to `TASK-260619-spid7i` as `TASK-260619-spid7i_foreground-harness-postmortem.md`.

---

## Template-Based Prompt Composition

Spawn always composes the prompt from the role template:

1. Reads task README (title, description, scope, acceptance criteria)
2. Reads precondition resources from progress.md
3. Loads each resource's content from `.task-board/.resources/`
4. Renders everything through a Go template

The resolved `agent_context.profile` selects one of two embedded templates:

- `full` is the backward-compatible default. It renders the historical role
  body, CLI reference, run-control, saving, safety, completion, and workdir
  sections unchanged.
- `lite` renders a compact invariant/command role contract. It keeps the exact
  FIRST/LAST lifecycle commands, merged task+role DoD, lazy skill catalog, task
  metadata/preconditions, task-scoped outcome command, literal `to-review`
  handoff wording, reviewer verdict branches, compact Stop-The-Line evidence
  packet, and workdir. Selection, checklist injection, completion-hook evidence
  enforcement, run tracking, and reviewer routing remain board/runtime gates
  outside the presentation template.

Because a lite worker receives one concrete element and its task inputs, its
prompt uses compact task-specific projections and skips routine `summary()`,
`plan()`, `schema()`, and `{ full }`. Scoped schema remains available after an
unknown call; broad routing and audit reads remain Orchestrator/full-profile
tools.

The **full default template** is embedded in the binary and includes:
- Task metadata (ID, title, description, scope, AC, parent)
- Precondition resource contents as "Instructions" sections
- Board CLI reference (status updates, checklist, notes, resource saving)
- Explicit instructions to save results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` for revisions
- Task-scoped artifact naming guidance such as `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`
- Logbook guidance for important findings, decisions, anomalies, or regressions
- Completion-hook contract: if a spawned task lands in `to-review` without a new task-scoped outcome artifact, the hook leaves a blocking note and reverts the task to its start status

**Project-local template overrides** are profile-specific:

- `.agents/spawn-template.md` for `full`
- `.agents/spawn-template-lite.md` for `lite`

Both expose the same data:
```
{{.TaskID}}                    — element ID
{{.TaskTitle}}                 — from README title
{{.TaskDescription}}           — from README description
{{.TaskScope}}                 — from README scope
{{.TaskAC}}                    — from README acceptance criteria
{{.ParentID}}                  — parent element ID
{{.AgentName}}                 — agent display name (e.g. "[implementer] developer (claude)")
{{.BoardDir}}                  — path to .task-board/
{{.PreconditionResources}}     — slice of {Name, Description, Content}
{{.RoleName}}                  — role name (e.g. "developer") — empty if no --role
{{.RoleDescription}}           — compact description from role.md frontmatter
{{.RoleBody}}                  — markdown body from role.md
{{.Archetype}}                 — "implementer", "analyst", "reviewer", "tester"
{{.Skills}}                    — slice of {Name, Path} — resolved lazy skill catalog refs
{{.StartStatus}}               — status to set on launch (e.g. "development")
{{.EndStatus}}                 — status to set on completion (e.g. "to-review")
{{.DoDContent}}                — merged DoD: task-specific board checklist + role baseline DoD
```

**Workflow for spawning:**
```bash
# 1. Put task-specific DoD on the board
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text="OAuth2 flow works end-to-end")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text="Token refresh handles edge cases")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text="Rate limiting returns 429")'

# 2. Link precondition resources (optional — provides additional context)
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, type=precondition, content="...", description="Implementation guide")'

# 3. Spawn with explicit role/model/reasoning selection
task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max

# 4. Agent runs, saves results
# (agent runs: task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="...", description="Implementation notes")')
```

